#! ia 1.0

@decision public-catalogue-cut
  meaning
    says "inventarch/marketplace, catalogue name inventarch, is the public InventArch plugin catalogue. It was cut on 2026-10-30 from the tree of InventArch's internal plugin catalogue at dd993c0, with fresh history: the first commit imports that tree unchanged so the removal is reviewable, and the next removes everything that is not public. It ships one plugin, the inventarch core, and the public kits ia-method, ia-kb and ia-authoring."
  work
    title "Cut the public catalogue with fresh history"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What is this repository, and where did it come from?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "inventarch/marketplace, catalogue name inventarch, is the public InventArch plugin catalogue. It was cut on 2026-10-30 from the tree of InventArch's internal plugin catalogue at dd993c0, with fresh history: the first commit imports that tree unchanged so the removal is reviewable, and the next removes everything that is not public. It ships one plugin, the inventarch core, and the public kits ia-method, ia-kb and ia-authoring."
    rationale "Public kits and their install machinery need a home anyone can read; proprietary methods stay in private sources and reach agents through an InventArch account."

@decision public-apache-license
  meaning
    says "Apache-2.0 for every file here, with LICENSE and NOTICE copied from inventarch/ia; every plugin manifest and kit descriptor says Apache-2.0."
  work
    title "License everything public under Apache-2.0"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Under which licence do the public plugin and kits ship?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Apache-2.0 for every file here, with LICENSE and NOTICE copied from inventarch/ia; every plugin manifest and kit descriptor says Apache-2.0."
    rationale "It matches the public IA framework the kits build on."

@decision public-kit-naming
  meaning
    says "Every kit: folder ia-<n>, kit id inventarch/ia-<n>-kit, main system ia-<n>, workspace ia-<n>-kit, distribution ia-<n>-distribution, steward ia-<n>-steward; a kit with several systems names the extra ones ia-<n>-<part>. Public or private is shown by the repository that holds a kit, never by its name."
  work
    title "Name every kit ia-<name>"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How are kits named?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Every kit: folder ia-<n>, kit id inventarch/ia-<n>-kit, main system ia-<n>, workspace ia-<n>-kit, distribution ia-<n>-distribution, steward ia-<n>-steward; a kit with several systems names the extra ones ia-<n>-<part>. Public or private is shown by the repository that holds a kit, never by its name."
    rationale "One pattern makes a kit's records predictable from its name, and moving a kit between repositories never renames it."

@decision public-default-kits
  meaning
    says "The core bundles exactly three kits: ia-method for Work (the operating loop, the provisional-foundation ceiling, and one method capability with a four-cell playbook per public record word, governance words read-and-apply only), ia-kb for Knowledge (placement, binding, maintenance, checks, migration and review, with the kb-workspace-location convention), and ia-authoring for Delivery (record, host and catalogue authoring as one process). The retired ids inventarch/host-authoring-kit and inventarch/catalogue-authoring-kit are replaced by ia-authoring."
  work
    title "Carry ia-method, ia-kb and ia-authoring as the lane defaults"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which kits does the public core carry, and which lane does each cover?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The core bundles exactly three kits: ia-method for Work (the operating loop, the provisional-foundation ceiling, and one method capability with a four-cell playbook per public record word, governance words read-and-apply only), ia-kb for Knowledge (placement, binding, maintenance, checks, migration and review, with the kb-workspace-location convention), and ia-authoring for Delivery (record, host and catalogue authoring as one process). The retired ids inventarch/host-authoring-kit and inventarch/catalogue-authoring-kit are replaced by ia-authoring."
    rationale "Each lane needs a public default; host and catalogue authoring must be one consistent process with record authoring."

@decision public-opal-per-lane
  meaning
    says "Each lane's entry capability (work.*, knowledge.*, delivery.*) is declared whole in its kit's delivery.json, so its packet carries all four cells; knowledge place maps to the plan phase; per-activity keys keep their one-phase scope keys, and --phase overrides. No word is minted: phases are values of the public phase word."
  work
    title "Make each lane entry a whole four-phase procedure"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How is the orient, plan, act, learn loop represented in each lane?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "Each lane's entry capability (work.*, knowledge.*, delivery.*) is declared whole in its kit's delivery.json, so its packet carries all four cells; knowledge place maps to the plan phase; per-activity keys keep their one-phase scope keys, and --phase overrides. No word is minted: phases are values of the public phase word."
    rationale "A request with no specific activity gets the whole loop; a specific activity gets the cell it needs."

@decision public-releases-format-2
  meaning
    says "releases.json is a small index (format 2: kit, latest, and one row per release naming releases/<version>.json where its records were); each per-version file holds that release's record names and is immutable once published. Conversion is lossless (expanding the index rebuilds format 1 byte for byte), the consistency guard compares both sides through one expand(), new rows add source {repository, commit}, and the history is left out of the implementation digest and the method closure capture."
  work
    title "Split kit release history per version"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How are a kit's releases recorded?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "releases.json is a small index (format 2: kit, latest, and one row per release naming releases/<version>.json where its records were); each per-version file holds that release's record names and is immutable once published. Conversion is lossless (expanding the index rebuilds format 1 byte for byte), the consistency guard compares both sides through one expand(), new rows add source {repository, commit}, and the history is left out of the implementation digest and the method closure capture."
    rationale "The embedded record lists made one file tens of thousands of lines long and changed every pin at every release."

@decision public-kit-lookup
  meaning
    says "The core resolves a kit from its own config/kits, then from <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/, filled by scripts/kit.mjs add --from <folder or archive> today and by the InventArch API later. The user verbs are list, add, bind, upgrade, repin, rebind and check in plugins/ia/scripts/kit.mjs; the maintainer verbs are pack, bundle, index and convert in tools/kit.mjs; every write validates first and previews unless --apply. kits/index.json broadcasts each kit's latest release and the binding keys it offers."
  work
    title "Look kits up in the plugin, then in the IA home"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where does the core find a kit?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "The core resolves a kit from its own config/kits, then from <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/, filled by scripts/kit.mjs add --from <folder or archive> today and by the InventArch API later. The user verbs are list, add, bind, upgrade, repin, rebind and check in plugins/ia/scripts/kit.mjs; the maintainer verbs are pack, bundle, index and convert in tools/kit.mjs; every write validates first and previews unless --apply. kits/index.json broadcasts each kit's latest release and the binding keys it offers."
    rationale "Kits served outside the plugin need an install location the resolver trusts, and repositories need one tool to upgrade, repin and rebind after a release."

@decision public-account-offer
  meaning
    says "At session startup only, once per session, never blocking, in one short message, the core tells the user how to add the remote InventArch MCP server (https://api.inventarch.dev/mcp); the host runs OAuth with PKCE, and the plugin stores no token and makes no network call. An account counts as present when the host already lists an inventarch MCP server in its documented local configuration or a design binding is set in the environment. The offer is suppressed by 'accountOffer': false in ~/.ia/config.json, by IA_NO_ACCOUNT_OFFER=1, or by a non-empty CI."
  work
    title "Offer an InventArch account once per session"
    status superseded
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the public core offer an account?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "At session startup only, once per session, never blocking, in one short message, the core tells the user how to add the remote InventArch MCP server (https://api.inventarch.dev/mcp); the host runs OAuth with PKCE, and the plugin stores no token and makes no network call. An account counts as present when the host already lists an inventarch MCP server in its documented local configuration or a design binding is set in the environment. The offer is suppressed by 'accountOffer': false in ~/.ia/config.json, by IA_NO_ACCOUNT_OFFER=1, or by a non-empty CI."
    rationale "Subscription kits need an account, and the offer must never get in the way of work."

@decision public-governance-probe
  meaning
    says "When .ia/governance.json exists and no installed kit declares governance handlers (a tool pre handler in its handlers.json), the session hook says once, at startup, that the repository is governed and to add the ia-governance kit (ia kit add). No plugin is looked for. It never falls back silently to ungoverned work."
  work
    title "Say when a governed repository lacks its governance kit"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What does the public core do in a repository adopted for governance?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "When .ia/governance.json exists and no installed kit declares governance handlers (a tool pre handler in its handlers.json), the session hook says once, at startup, that the repository is governed and to add the ia-governance kit (ia kit add). No plugin is looked for. It never falls back silently to ungoverned work."
    rationale "Governance is not part of the public offering, and pretending a governed repository is ungoverned would be worse than saying so."

@decision public-workspace-placement
  meaning
    says "User-facing workspaces default to ~/Documents/ia/<domain> (studio, music, design and so on), and the user can point elsewhere. ~/.ia is the IA home only: config, state, the location registry and installed kits. A knowledge repository holds one .ia/src: a personal or single-team KB has one @workspace; an organisation store has one @workspace per department, each declaring its scope folders, plus an organisation @workspace that also declares the repository root (. @authored); a workspaces/<name>/ folder is only for a workspace released or adopted on its own."
  work
    title "Place user workspaces under ~/Documents/ia"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where do a user's workspaces live?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "User-facing workspaces default to ~/Documents/ia/<domain> (studio, music, design and so on), and the user can point elsewhere. ~/.ia is the IA home only: config, state, the location registry and installed kits. A knowledge repository holds one .ia/src: a personal or single-team KB has one @workspace; an organisation store has one @workspace per department, each declaring its scope folders, plus an organisation @workspace that also declares the repository root (. @authored); a workspaces/<name>/ folder is only for a workspace released or adopted on its own."
    rationale "The CLI refuses a workspace inside its home, and a predictable default keeps workspaces easy to find. The knowledge-repository layout is the operator's ruling dec-132 (2026-10-31), which replaced the earlier one-workspace-at-the-root wording."

@decision public-plugin-components
  meaning
    says "Skills, agents, references, templates, examples, config, node scripts and tests. No LSP servers, commands, settings, monitors or bin/, no MCP server but the core's IA door (public-door-mcp), and no hooks, mcpServers or permissionMode in skill or agent frontmatter. At most four model-invocable skills per plugin; stage-skill descriptions at most 160 characters; every agent declares a tools allowlist. The only hooks are the core's qualified set (public-core-hooks)."
  work
    title "Admit only reviewable plugin components"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What may a plugin in this catalogue ship?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "Skills, agents, references, templates, examples, config, node scripts and tests. No LSP servers, commands, settings, monitors or bin/, no MCP server but the core's IA door (public-door-mcp), and no hooks, mcpServers or permissionMode in skill or agent frontmatter. At most four model-invocable skills per plugin; stage-skill descriptions at most 160 characters; every agent declares a tools allowlist. The only hooks are the core's qualified set (public-core-hooks)."
    rationale "A Git install copies a plugin's whole folder, so everything that ships must be reviewable text with no ambient authority."

@decision public-core-hooks
  meaning
    says "The SessionStart bootstrap (startup, resume and compact, scripts/bootstrap.mjs, 15 seconds) prints the workspace identity and the resolve instruction, makes the account offer at startup and the governance probe, re-injects the current task pin after a compaction, reads no credentials and never fetches. scripts/handlers.mjs runs the handlers installed kits declare, with one event argument: SessionStart for every source (60 seconds), UserPromptSubmit (30), PreToolUse (30) and PostToolUse (60) on Write, Edit, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit, Bash and PowerShell, and Stop (30). tools/validate.py pins all of them to exactly these events, matchers, commands and timeouts, and the Codex manifest keeps them off."
  work
    title "Pin the core's hooks"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which hooks does the core ship, and how are they held?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "The SessionStart bootstrap (startup, resume and compact, scripts/bootstrap.mjs, 15 seconds) prints the workspace identity and the resolve instruction, makes the account offer at startup and the governance probe, re-injects the current task pin after a compaction, reads no credentials and never fetches. scripts/handlers.mjs runs the handlers installed kits declare, with one event argument: SessionStart for every source (60 seconds), UserPromptSubmit (30), PreToolUse (30) and PostToolUse (60) on Write, Edit, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit, Bash and PowerShell, and Stop (30). tools/validate.py pins all of them to exactly these events, matchers, commands and timeouts, and the Codex manifest keeps them off."
    rationale "Hooks run without a prompt, so each one is narrow, read-only and pinned."

@decision public-codex-manifest-interface
  meaning
    says "A Codex manifest is the Claude manifest without displayName and dependencies, plus skills and Codex's interface block (displayName, shortDescription, category); a hook plugin adds the empty hook configuration that keeps its hooks off in Codex. Every plugin ships a CODEX.md, and every skill opens with a bridge line to it."
  work
    title "Derive the Codex manifest from the Claude one"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How do the two host manifests relate?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A Codex manifest is the Claude manifest without displayName and dependencies, plus skills and Codex's interface block (displayName, shortDescription, category); a hook plugin adds the empty hook configuration that keeps its hooks off in Codex. Every plugin ships a CODEX.md, and every skill opens with a bridge line to it."
    rationale "Codex reads display fields from the interface block and lists every skill."

@decision public-local-gates
  meaning
    says "npm ci installs lefthook, Biome and the pinned @inventarch/cli; lefthook runs validate, selection --check, bundle --check, consistency, lint and the language check before each commit, and again before each push with consistency --base origin/main. CI runs the same and the test suites."
  work
    title "Run the gates before every commit and push"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which checks guard this repository, and where do they run?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "npm ci installs lefthook, Biome and the pinned @inventarch/cli; lefthook runs validate, selection --check, bundle --check, consistency, lint and the language check before each commit, and again before each push with consistency --base origin/main. CI runs the same and the test suites."
    rationale "Problems are cheapest to fix before they leave the machine."

@decision public-ci-matrix
  meaning
    says "Ubuntu for every pull request into main; Ubuntu, Windows and macOS for every push to main, including the native qualification shards."
  work
